---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../components/Math.astro';
import { site, personNode } from '../data/site';

const softwareSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'SMVac',
  description:
    'A modular C++ framework for studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model: NNLO matching, 3-loop RG evolution, the RG-improved one-loop effective potential, and a semi-analytical estimate of the vacuum decay action on a Fubini–Lipatov trial profile.',
  codeRepository: site.smvac,
  author: personNode,
  programmingLanguage: ['C++', 'Python'],
  dateModified: '2026-10-02',
};
---
<Base
  title="SMVac · electroweak vacuum stability"
  description="SMVac: a C++ framework for studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model. 3-loop RG evolution, NNLO matching, the RG-improved one-loop effective potential, and a semi-analytical Fubini–Lipatov trial-profile estimate of the decay action, with phase diagrams in the (Mh, Mt) plane."
  jsonLd={[softwareSchema]}

  ogImage="/og/smvac.png">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research software · C++ / Python</p>
      <h1>SMVac</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 42rem;">
        A numerical study of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        <strong style="color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600;">Naman Goyal</strong> · May 2024 – May 2026 ·
        Mentored by Prof. Rick Sandeepan Gupta (TIFR) ·
        <a href={site.smvac} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac-Research</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
      <h2 id="overview-heading" style="margin-top: 1rem;"><span class="secno">01</span> Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The Standard Model of particle physics appears, from its measured parameters, to live on a
        knife's edge. At field values far beyond any collider, the effective Higgs potential turns
        over: the quartic coupling <Math tex={'\\lambda(\\mu)'} />, run up in energy, becomes negative
        at scales of order <Math tex={'10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. The electroweak vacuum in which
        we live is then not the absolute minimum of the theory but a <em>metastable</em> one,
        destined in principle to decay by quantum tunneling. Whether that decay can ever matter is
        controlled by the semiclassical bounce action <Math tex={'S'} />: the vacuum survives
        cosmological timescales provided <Math tex={'S'} /> is large enough. The measured Higgs and
        top masses place our universe uncomfortably close to the boundary where this guarantee fails.
      </p>
      <p>
        SMVac is the computational framework I developed to study this problem quantitatively. It
        combines precision renormalization group evolution with the semiclassical
        Fubini–Lipatov (conformal) ansatz: rather than assuming a constant quartic coupling, the
        code evaluates the Euclidean action on the conformal field profile with the full running,
        one-loop effective potential, and minimizes it over the profile scale. The bounce equation
        itself is never integrated, so the result is a <strong>trial-profile action
        estimate</strong>, not the bounce action; the most robust output is the stability boundary,
        which does not depend on the ansatz.
      </p>

      <dl class="findings">
        <div>
          <dt>Established</dt>
          <dd>
            The absolute-stability boundary:
            <Math tex={'M_h^{\\rm crit} = 129.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> at
            <Math tex={'M_t = 173.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, independent of the trial profile.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Estimated</dt>
          <dd>
            The decay action at the benchmark point,
            <Math tex={'S_{\\rm trial} = 2168.7'} /> against a threshold of
            <Math tex={'484'} />: metastable, with a lifetime far beyond the age of the universe.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Not established</dt>
          <dd>
            How the trial-profile action relates to the exact bounce action. No inequality between
            them is shown, and no independent bounce computation was performed.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading"><span class="secno">02</span> The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Why the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane? The top quark drives <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> down
        through the RG flow, the Higgs mass pushes it up; the stability verdict is therefore a curve
        in the Higgs–top mass plane, and the experimental point
        <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t) \\approx (125.1, 173.1)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> sits close to it.
        Classic analyses (Buttazzo et al. 2013) mapped this boundary with multi-loop precision.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard analytical shortcut evaluates the decay rate in the strict Fubini–Lipatov
        limit, where the potential is assumed purely quartic,
        <Math tex={'V \\approx \\tfrac{1}{4}\\lambda\\,\\phi^4'} /> with <em>constant</em>
        <Math tex={'\\lambda < 0'} /> at high scales. Near the metastability boundary the running
        of the couplings is exactly what this shortcut ignores. SMVac asks whether that matters:
        how far the action evaluated with the running potential departs from the strict conformal
        estimate, and what drives the difference.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
      <h2 id="method-heading"><span class="secno">03</span> Method</h2>
      <p>The computational pipeline, as implemented in the repository:</p>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>Input <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> and <Math tex={'\\alpha_s(M_Z)'} /></li>
        <li>NNLO matching at the top scale <Math tex={'M_t'} /> (Buttazzo et al. 2013, linearized)</li>
        <li>3-loop RG evolution to the Planck scale (RK4)</li>
        <li>Locate <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\min}'} /> and the instability scale <Math tex={'\\mu_{\\rm inst}'} /></li>
        <li>Trial-profile action over the Fubini–Lipatov conformal profile</li>
        <li>Optimize the profile scale <Math tex={'R'} /> (Golden Section Search)</li>
        <li>Classify via the Coleman criterion <Math tex={'\\Gamma/V \\sim t_U^{-4}'} /></li>
        <li>Repeat over an <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> grid → phase diagram</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Effective potential</h3>
      <p>
        The tree-level potential is improved by the running coupling, with one-loop
        Coleman–Weinberg corrections from the top, <Math tex={'W'} />, and <Math tex={'Z'} /> fields,
        in the high-field approximation where the false vacuum sits at the origin:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi) \\;\\approx\\; \\tfrac{1}{4}\\,\\lambda_{\\rm eff}(\\mu)\\,\\phi^{4} \\;+\\; \\Delta V_{\\rm 1\\text{-}loop}(\\phi), \\qquad \\mu \\sim \\phi.'}
      />
      <p>
        At large field values the potential is regularized by a Planck-suppressed
        <Math tex={'\\phi^6/M_{\\rm Pl}^2'} /> operator whose coefficient is an assumption,
        <Math tex={'c_6 = 1'} /> by default; nothing in the code predicts it.
      </p>

      <h3>Renormalization group evolution</h3>
      <p>
        The couplings <Math tex={'(g_1, g_2, g_3, y_t, \\lambda)'} />, with
        <Math tex={'y_b, y_\\tau'} /> subleading, are evolved with their three-loop beta functions
        by fixed-step RK4 integration in <Math tex={'t = 2\\ln\\mu'} />, from the matching scale
        <Math tex={'M_t'} /> to the Planck scale:
      </p>
      <Math block tex={'\\frac{d g_i}{d \\ln \\mu} \\;=\\; \\beta_{g_i}^{\\rm (3\\text{-}loop)}.'} />

      <h3>Semiclassical decay action</h3>
      <p>
        SMVac retains the analytical Fubini–Lipatov field profile,
        <Math tex={"\\phi_{\\rm FL}(r) = \\sqrt{2/|\\lambda_R|}\\; 2R^{2}/(r^{2}+R^{2})"} />, but
        evaluates the Euclidean action along it with the running potential: the kinetic term is
        closed-form in the conformal limit, while the potential term is integrated numerically over
        the full <Math tex={'V_{\\rm eff}'} /> (composite Simpson's rule, <Math tex={'N = 2048'} />
        points), and the profile radius <Math tex={'R'} /> is optimized by Golden Section Search to
        tolerance <Math tex={'10^{-13}'} />:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'S \\;=\\; \\underbrace{\\frac{16\\pi^{2}}{3\\,|\\lambda_R|}}_{\\rm closed\\ form} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{2\\pi^{2}\\!\\int_0^{\\infty}\\! dr\\; r^{3}\\,\\bigl[V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi_{\\rm FL}) - V_{\\rm eff}(0)\\bigr]}_{\\rm numerical}, \\qquad \\frac{\\Gamma}{V} \\sim A\\,e^{-S}.'}
      />
      <p>
        A point of the plane is classified <em>stable</em> if <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> never turns
        negative, <em>unstable</em> if <Math tex={'S'} /> falls below the universe-age threshold
        <Math tex={'S_{\\rm th} \\approx 4\\ln(t_U\\,\\mu_{\\rm inst})'} /> with
        <Math tex={'t_U = 10\\,\\mathrm{Gyr}'} />, and <em>metastable</em> otherwise. A
        perturbativity guard flags regions where couplings exceed <Math tex={'4\\pi'} />, and points
        where the ansatz breaks down carry an explicit flag instead of a physical verdict.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="results-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">04</span> Results</h2>
        <p>
          Production datasets are evaluated point by point with no interpolation: the full
          <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane at <Math tex={'0.5\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> resolution
          (501 × 501 points), the phenomenologically relevant window at
          <Math tex={'0.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, and a boundary strip at
          <Math tex={'0.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />.
        </p>
        <p>
          The absolute-stability boundary sits at
          <Math tex={'M_h^{\\rm crit} = 129.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> for
          <Math tex={'M_t = 173.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. At the benchmark point
          <Math tex={'(125.1, 173.1)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> the vacuum is <em>metastable</em>:
          <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\rm eff}'} /> crosses zero at
          <Math tex={'\\mu_1 \\simeq 7.6 \\times 10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> and the optimized
          trial-profile action is <Math tex={'S_{\\rm trial} = 2168.7'} /> against a threshold of
          <Math tex={'484'} />, consistent with the literature consensus that the SM lifetime
          exceeds the age of the universe.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/01_full_phase_diagram.png"
        alt="Phase diagram of the Standard Model vacuum across the Higgs-top mass plane from 0 to 250 GeV. Green marks absolute stability, yellow metastability, red instability, and black the non-perturbative region; thin contours show constant action, and the star marks the benchmark point (125.1, 173.1) GeV inside the metastable band, close to the stability boundary."
        width={2220}
        height={1920}
        label="Fig. 1"
        caption="Classification of the SM vacuum over the full (M_h, M_t) plane (trial-profile estimator, 0.5 GeV grid). Solid line: the λ_min = 0 stability boundary; the star marks (125.1, 173.1) GeV. Dots along the boundary are ansatz-breakdown points, a documented artifact of the fixed trial profile, not physical instabilities. The NNLO matching is linearized around (125.15, 173.34) GeV, so regions far from that window are extrapolations of the fit, not SM predictions. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/02_phenomenological_zoom.png"
        alt="Zoom of the stability phase diagram onto the window from 112 to 138 GeV in Higgs mass and 155 to 185 GeV in top mass, at 0.1 GeV resolution, with the PDG 2022 region around the experimental point and constant-action contours."
        width={2100}
        height={1680}
        label="Fig. 2"
        caption="The phenomenologically relevant window at 0.1 GeV resolution, with the PDG 2022 region and constant-action contours. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Former anchor of the next section; kept so existing links still land on it. -->
    <span id="analytical-vs-rg-improved" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <section class="section" id="conformal-vs-trial" aria-labelledby="cvt-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="cvt-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">05</span> Conformal estimate vs. trial profile</h2>
        <p>
          The trial-profile action can be compared with the strict conformal estimate
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm conformal} = 8\\pi^2 / (3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} />. The comparison is
          between two approximations, not a correctness statement. With the Planck operator
          switched off (<Math tex={'c_6 = 0'} />), the two agree to about <Math tex={'1\\%'} /> or
          better everywhere tested: <Math tex={'-0.08\\%'} /> at the benchmark point and
          <Math tex={'-0.9\\%'} /> at <Math tex={'(134.75, 176.5)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> near the
          boundary. Inside this trial family, RG improvement barely moves the action.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assumed <Math tex={'c_6 = 1'} /> operator raises the action by up to
          <Math tex={'+33\\%'} /> near the boundary, and that is what drives the classification
          differences there. An earlier version of this analysis attributed the near-boundary
          difference of about <Math tex={'30\\%'} /> to RG improvement; the
          <Math tex={'c_6 = 0'} /> comparison shows that attribution was incorrect.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/05_conformal_vs_rg_difference.png"
        alt="Classification difference map over the full Higgs-top plane. Most of the plane is grey, where the strict conformal estimate and the trial-profile evaluation agree; thin blue and orange bands along the classification boundaries mark points classified more or less stable by the trial-profile evaluation, and black marks breakdown or out-of-validity points."
        width={2220}
        height={1920}
        label="Fig. 3"
        caption="Classification difference map: where the strict conformal estimate and the trial-profile evaluation disagree over the full plane. Differences concentrate along the classification boundaries and are driven mainly by the assumed c6 = 1 Planck operator and by ansatz breakdown. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/06_action_fractional_difference.png"
        alt="Two heatmaps of the fractional difference between the trial-profile and conformal actions over the phenomenological window. Left, with c6 = 0, the map is uniformly near zero. Right, with c6 = 1, the difference grows to about +30 percent along the stability boundary."
        width={3480}
        height={1410}
        label="Fig. 4"
        caption="Fractional difference (S_trial − S_conformal)/S_conformal over the phenomenological window, without (c6 = 0, left) and with (c6 = 1, right) the assumed Planck operator. At c6 = 0 the two actions agree to within about 1%; the large near-boundary differences at c6 = 1 come from the assumed operator, not from RG running. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="validation" aria-labelledby="validation-heading">
      <h2 id="validation-heading"><span class="secno">06</span> Validation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Literature consistency.</strong> The computed boundary,
          <Math tex={'M_h^{\\rm crit} = 129.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> at
          <Math tex={'M_t = 173.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, sits within the theory uncertainty of the
          NNLO value <Math tex={'129.4 \\pm 1.0\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> of Degrassi et al. (2012) at the
          same inputs. This is a consistency check, not a precision validation: the code omits the
          two-loop effective potential and three-loop QCD threshold pieces of that analysis and
          linearizes the matching.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test suite.</strong> Eleven tests span unit checks of the numerics and matching,
          physics tests against analytic limits and the literature boundary, and regression tests
          against frozen reference tables (<code>reference/v1.1/</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resolution convergence.</strong> Committed datasets at
          <Math tex={'0.5'} />, <Math tex={'0.1'} />, and <Math tex={'0.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />
          come with a convergence-check script; every classification pixel corresponds to an
          actually evaluated grid point, with no interpolation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reproducibility.</strong> Datasets, figures, and reference tables are all
          regenerable from the committed scripts; nothing depends on machine-specific state.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <h2 id="limitations-heading"><span class="secno">07</span> Limitations</h2>
      <p>Stated plainly, because they bound what the results mean:</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem;">
          The full list is in the repository's <code>docs/limitations.md</code>; the
          absolute-stability boundary is the robust output throughout. The most important items:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No bounce-equation solve.</strong> The action is evaluated on a restricted
            trial-profile family and minimized over its scale. The bounce is a saddle point of the
            action, not a minimum over general field configurations, so the trial-profile action
            can lie above or below the true bounce action; its accuracy is unknown.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>High-field potential.</strong> The tree-level mass term is dropped, so the false
            vacuum sits at the origin rather than at the electroweak vacuum; the action describes
            tunneling from the origin as a proxy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Assumed Planck operator.</strong> The coefficient of the
            <Math tex={'\\phi^6'} /> term is set to <Math tex={'c_6 = 1'} /> by assumption, and it
            dominates the near-boundary action. With negative <Math tex={'c_6'} /> the trial action
            becomes unbounded below along the profile family.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Prefactor scale.</strong> The decay-rate prefactor uses the zero-crossing scale
            <Math tex={'\\mu_1'} /> instead of <Math tex={'1/R_*'} />, worth about
            <Math tex={'+47'} /> in <Math tex={'S_{\\rm th}'} /> at the benchmark point; the gauge
            dependence of <Math tex={'\\mu_1'} /> is not assessed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Matching and running.</strong> The NNLO matching is linearized, with
            <Math tex={'\\alpha_s'} />, <Math tex={'M_W'} />, and <Math tex={'M_b'} /> held fixed,
            and the four-loop <Math tex={'g_3'} /> running is incomplete.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-heading">
      <h2 id="code-heading"><span class="secno">08</span> Code</h2>
      <p>
        The current research codebase,
        <a href={site.smvac} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SMVac-Research</a>, is a
        CMake C++17 project: a library (RGE, effective potential, bounce, numerics), command-line
        apps for single-point benchmarks and phase-diagram scans, an eleven-test suite, committed
        example datasets, and scripts that regenerate every figure from the committed data with no
        interpolation. The original working repository,
        <a href={site.smvacOriginal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SMVac</a>, remains
        preserved unchanged as the provenance record.
      </p>
      <pre class="codeblock"><code>git clone https://github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac-Research.git
cd SMVac-Research
cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
./build/benchmark_point 125.1 173.1</code></pre>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        If you use SMVac, please cite it via the repository's
        <a href={`${site.smvac}/blob/main/CITATION.cff`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CITATION.cff</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="future" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading"><span class="secno">09</span> Future work</h2>
      <p>
        The documented limitations mark the natural next steps: a full bounce-equation solve, which
        would also settle how the trial-profile estimate relates to the true action; a decay-rate
        prefactor that retains the radius dependence; and a provenance check of the Planck-operator
        coefficient.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
